\newcommand{\delta}{\delta}
\newcommand{\rho}{\rho}

\begin{definition}\label{}
 A {\em{(deterministic) tree decomposition automaton}} of {\em{width}} $\ell$
 consists of 
 \begin{itemize}
  \item a {\em{state set}} $Q$;
  \item a set of {\em{accepting states}} $F\subseteq Q$;
  \item an {\em{initial mapping}} $\iota$ that maps every boundaried graph $G$ on at most $\ell+1$ vertices to a state $\iota(G)\in Q$; and
  \item a {\em{transition mapping}} $\delta$ that maps every $7$-tuple of form $(B,X,Y,Z,J,q',q'')$, where $B\subseteq \Omega$ is a set of size at most $\ell+1$, $X,Y,Z\subseteq B$, $J\in \binom{B}{2}\setminus \binom{X}{2}$, $q'\in Q$, and $q'' \in Q \cup \{\bot\}$ to a state $\delta(B,X,Y,Z,J,q',q'')\in Q$.
 \end{itemize}
 The {\em{run}} of a tree decomposition automaton $\mathcal{A}$ on a binary boundaried tree decomposition $(T,\ensuremath{\mathtt{bag}},\ensuremath{\mathtt{edges}})$ of a boundaried graph $G$ is the unique labelling $\rho_\mathcal{A}\colon V(T)\to Q$ satisfying the following properties:
 \begin{itemize}
  \item For every leaf $l$ of $T$, we have $$\rho_\mathcal{A}(l)=\iota(G_l).$$
  \item For every non-leaf node $x$ of $T$ with one child $y$, we have
  $$\rho_\mathcal{A}(x)=\delta(\ensuremath{\mathtt{bag}}(x),\adhesion{x},\adhesion{y},\emptyset,\ensuremath{\mathtt{edges}}(x),\rho_\mathcal{A}(y),\bot).$$
  \item For every non-leaf node $x$ of $T$ with two children $y$ and $z$, we have
  $$\rho_\mathcal{A}(x)=\delta(\ensuremath{\mathtt{bag}}(x),\adhesion{x},\adhesion{y},\adhesion{z},\ensuremath{\mathtt{edges}}(x),\rho_\mathcal{A}(y),\rho_\mathcal{A}(z)).$$
 \end{itemize}
 A tree decomposition automaton $\mathcal{A}$ {\em{accepts}} $(T,\ensuremath{\mathtt{bag}},\ensuremath{\mathtt{edges}})$ if $\rho_\mathcal{A}(r)\in F$, where $r$ is the root of $T$.
\end{definition}

Note that in the transitions described above, nodes with one child are treated by passing a ``dummy state'' $\bot \notin Q$ to the transition function instead of a state. Note that this allows $\delta$ also to recognize when there is only one child.
Also, the automata model presented above could in principle distinguish the left child $y$ from the right child $z$ and treat states passed from them differently. However, this will never be the case in our applications: in all constructed automata, the transition mapping will be symmetric with respect to swapping the role of the children $y$ and $z$.

\medskip

We say that a tree decomposition automaton $\mathcal{A}$ has {\em{evaluation time}} $\tau$ if functions $\iota$ and $\delta$ can be evaluated on any tuple of their arguments in time $\tau$, and moreover for a given $q\in Q$ it can be decided whether $q\in F$ in time $\tau$. Note that we do {\em{not}} require the state space $Q$ to be finite. In fact, in most of our applications it will be infinite, but we will be able to efficiently represent and manipulate the states.

We will often run a tree decomposition automaton on a non-boundaried annotated tree decomposition of a non-boundaried graph $G$. In such cases, we simply apply all the above definitions while treating $G$ as a boundaried graph with an empty boundary.

We will also use {\em{nondeterministic tree decomposition automata}}, which are defined just like in \cref{}, except that $\iota$ and $\delta$ are the {\em{initial relation}} and the {\em{transition relation}}, instead of mappings. That is, $\iota$ is a relation consisting of pairs of the form $(G,q)$, where $G$ is a boundaried graph on at most $\ell+1$ vertices, and $q\in Q$. Similarly, $\delta$ is a relation consisting of pairs of the form $((B,X,Y,Z,J,q',q''),q)$, where $(B,X,Y,Z,J,q',q'')$ is a $7$-tuple like in the domain of the transition mapping, and $q\in Q$. Then a run of a nondeterministic automaton $\mathcal{A}$ on a boundaried binary tree decomposition $(T,\ensuremath{\mathtt{bag}},\ensuremath{\mathtt{edges}})$ is a labelling $\rho$ of the nodes of $T$ with states such that

