\begin{proposition}\label{}
Let $I,J$ are two disjoint subsets of $\{1,\ldots,N\}$. If $X=\{x_i\}_{i\in I}$ and $Y = \{x_j\}_{j\in J}$ then the event $\{\max X < \max Y\}$ is independent from the inside rankings of $X$ and $Y$: $\pi(X)$ and $\pi(Y)$ (See Definition \ref{}). Moreover 
\[
\mathbf P(\max X < \max Y)
= \frac{|Y|}{|X| + |Y|}.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
The event $\{\max X < \max Y\}$ can be written as $\{\forall x \in X: \,x < \max Y\}$, which is independent of the inside-ranking of $X$, because reshuffling $X$ does not have any impact on the event.\\ 
Since $(x_1,\ldots,x_N)$ is randomly permuted, every element in $X \cup Y$ has the same probability of being the maximum in $X \cup Y$, therefore
\begin{align*}
\mathbf P(\max X < \max Y)
&= \mathbf P(\max\{X\cup Y\} \in Y) 
= \sum_{y \in Y} \mathbf P(\max\{X\cup Y\} = y)
= \frac{|Y|}{|X| + |Y|}.
\end{align*}
\end{proof}


