\begin{lemma}\label{}
 For every integer $\ell$ and $\mathsf{CMSO}_2$ sentence $\varphi$ there exists a tree decomposition automaton $\mathcal{A}_{\ell,\varphi}$ of width $\ell$ with the following property: for any graph $G$ and its annotated binary tree decomposition $(T,\ensuremath{\mathtt{bag}},\ensuremath{\mathtt{edges}})$ of width at most $\ell$, $\mathcal{A}_{\ell,\varphi}$ accepts $(T,\ensuremath{\mathtt{bag}},\ensuremath{\mathtt{edges}})$ if and only if $G\models \varphi$. The evaluation time is bounded by $f(\ell,\varphi)$ for some computable function $f$.   
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
 Let $p$ be the {\em{rank}} of $\varphi$: the maximum among the quantifier rank of $\varphi$ and the moduli of modular predicates appearing in $\varphi$. Consider any finite $X\subseteq \Omega$ and let $\mathsf{CMSO}_2(X)$ consists of all $\mathsf{CMSO}_2$ sentences that can additionally use elements of $X$ as constants (formally, these are $\mathsf{CMSO}_2$ sentences over the signature of graphs enriched by adding every $x\in X$ as a constant).
 It is well-known (see e.g.~\cite[Exercise~6.11]{Immerman99}) that for a given $X$ as above, one can compute a set $\mathrm{Sentences}^p(X)$ consisting of at most $g(\ell,|X|)$ sentences of $\mathsf{CMSO}_2(X)$, for some computable $g$, such that for every $\psi\in \mathsf{CMSO}_2(X)$ of rank at most $p$, there is $\psi'\in \mathrm{Sentences}^p(X)$ that is equivalent to $\psi$ in the sense of being satisfied in exactly the same graphs containing $X$. Also, the mapping $\psi\mapsto \psi'$ is computable.

 For a given boundaried graph $G$ with $X=\partial G$, we define the {\em{$p$-type}} of $G$ as follows:
 $$\mathrm{tp}^p(G)=\{\psi\in \mathrm{Sentences}^p(X)~|~G\models \psi\}.$$
 Now, we construct automaton $\mathcal{A}=\mathcal{A}_{\ell,\varphi}$ so that for every boundaried binary tree decomposition $(T,\ensuremath{\mathtt{bags}},\ensuremath{\mathtt{edges}})$ of a graph $G$, the run of $\mathcal{A}$ on $(T,\ensuremath{\mathtt{bags}},\ensuremath{\mathtt{edges}})$ is as follows:
 $$\rho_{\mathcal{A}}(x)=\mathrm{tp}^p(G_x)\qquad\textrm{for all }x\in V(T).$$
 When constructing $\mathcal{A}$, the only non-trivial check is that one can define a suitable transition mapping $\delta$. For this, it suffices to show that for every node $x$ of $T$ with children $y$ and $z$, given types $\mathrm{tp}^p(G_y)$ and $\mathrm{tp}^p(G_z)$ together with the information about the bag of $x$ (consisting of $\ensuremath{\mathtt{bag}}(x)$, $\adhesion{x}$, $\adhesion{y}$, $\adhesion{z}$, and $\ensuremath{\mathtt{edges}}(x)$), one can compute the type $\mathrm{tp}^p(G_x)$; and same for nodes with one child. This follows from a standard argument involving Ehrenfeucht-Fra\"isse games, cf.~\cite{GroheK09,Makowsky04}.
\end{proof}



