In \S\ref{}, we derived a contrastive framework for Bayesian Self-Supervised Learning (BSSL), which we then used to develop a practical algorithm (\cref{algorithm}). We now provide some additional further details used for our practical method. 

To recap our framework, we use data augmentation and mini-batching to create a set of contrastive tasks $\{\mathcal{D}^c\}$. Further employing parameter sharing between the labels of each $\mathcal{D}^c$ and the labels of $\mathcal{D}^t$ allows us to condition on $\mathcal{D}^c$ when making predictions on $\mathcal{D}^t$, thereby improving downstream predictions.

