\subsection{Automata constructions}

In subsequent sections we will use several automata. 
We now present four automata constructions that we will use.

\paragraph*{Tree decomposition properties automata.} We first construct three very simple automata that are used in \cref{} for maintaining properties of the tree decomposition itself.

\begin{lemma}\label{}
For every $\ell\in \mathbb{N}$ there exists tree decomposition automata $\Hc_{\ell}$, $\mathcal{S}_{\ell}$, $\mathcal{C}_{\ell}$, each of width $\ell$, with the following properties: for any graph $G$, annotated binary tree decomposition $(T,\ensuremath{\mathtt{bag}},\ensuremath{\mathtt{edges}})$ of $G$ of width at most $\ell$, and any node $x$ of $T$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\rho_{\Hc_\ell}(x)$ is equal to $\ensuremath{\mathsf{height}}(T_x)$,
\item $\rho_{\mathcal{S}_\ell}(x)$ is equal to $|V(T_x)|$, and
\item $\rho_{\mathcal{C}_\ell}(x)$ is equal to $|\component{x}|$.
\end{itemize}
 The evaluation times of $\Hc_\ell$ and $\mathcal{S}_\ell$ are~$\Oh{1}$, and the evaluation time of $\mathcal{C}_\ell$ is~$\Oh{\ell}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The state sets of each of the automata are $\mathbb{N}$.
Let us define for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$ that $\max(n, \bot) = n$ and $n + \bot = n$.
For the height automaton $\Hc_\ell$, the initial mapping and the transition mapping are defined as follows (here $\_$ denotes any input value):
 \begin{eqnarray*}
  \iota(\_) & = & 1 \\
  \delta(\_,\_,\_,\_,\_,q',q'') & = & 1+\max(q',q'').
 \end{eqnarray*}
 For the size automaton $\mathcal{S}_\ell$, the initial mapping and the transition mapping are defined as follows:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 \iota(\_) & = & 1 \\
 \delta(\_,\_,\_,\_,\_,q',q'') & = & 1+q'+q''.
 \end{eqnarray*}
 For the $|\component{x}|$ automaton $\mathcal{C}_\ell$, the initial mapping and the transition mapping are defined as follows:
 \begin{eqnarray*}
 \iota(G) & = & |V(G) \setminus \partial G| \\
 \delta(B, X, \_, \_, \_, q', q'') & = & q' + q'' + |B \setminus X|.
 \end{eqnarray*}
 It is straightforward to see that these automata satisfy the required properties.
\end{proof}



