\section{Equivalence of Planted and Non-Planted \texorpdfstring{$k$}{}-SUM}\label{}

In this section, we prove an equivalence between planted and non-planted $k$-SUM at densities $\geq 1$ for any finite Abelian group. We first show that at $\Delta=1$, any algorithm that solves the planted problem can be used to solve the non-planted problem. The precise theorem we show is the following.

\begin{theorem}\label{}
    For any $k\in\Nat$ and ensemble $\G$ of density $1$, suppose there exists an algorithm that runs in time $T(r)$ and solves \emph{planted} search $k$-SUM over $\G$ with success probability at least $\epsilon(r)$. Then, the same algorithm also solves \emph{non-planted} search $k$-SUM over $\G$ with success probability at least $\epsilon(r)^{3\/2}\/9$.
\end{theorem}

