\begin{equation}
\label{e:ue}
u_t + ue = -\L_\a(\rho u) + f.
\end{equation}
Differentiating this, applying $\L_\a$ to the density equation, and subtracting, we obtain an evolution equation for $e$:
\begin{equation}
\label{e:econs}
e_t + (ue)' = f'.
\end{equation}
Next, we define $q:=e\/\rho$.  Taking the time derivative of $q$ and using the density equation, we see that $q$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
\label{e:qtrans}
q_t + uq' = \frac{f'}{\rho}.
\end{equation}
But then $q'$ satisfies an equation like \eqref{e:econs}:
\begin{equation}
\label{e:qxcons}
q'_t + (uq')' = (q_t + uq')' = \left( \frac{f'}{\rho} \right)'.
