And finally, $q'\/\rho$ satisfies an equation like \eqref{e:qtrans}:
\begin{equation}
\label{e:qxrhotrans}
\left( \frac{q'}{\rho} \right)_t + u \left( \frac{q'}{\rho} \right)' = \frac{1}{\rho} \left( \frac{f'}{\rho} \right)'.
\end{equation}
Obviously this process can be continued, but $q'\/\rho$ is the highest order quantity of this type that we make use of below.

